response:
 When Phil rolls 6 fair 6-sided dice, there are $6^6 = 46656$ possible outcomes. To calculate the probability of at least two dice showing the same number, we will first calculate the probability of all dice showing different numbers.
The first die can be any of the 6 numbers. The second die can be any of the remaining 5 numbers, the third die can be any of the remaining 4 numbers, and so on. Therefore, the number of ways to roll 6 dice such that each die shows a different number is $6 \cdot 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 = 720$.
So, the probability of rolling all 6 dice such that each die shows a different number is
}
$P(\text{all different}) = \frac{720}{46656} = \frac{5}{324}$
}
The probability of at least two dice showing the same number is the complement of the probability of all dice showing different numbers:
$P(\text{at least two same}) = 1 - P(\text{all different}) = 1 - \frac{5}{324} = \frac{319}{324}$
}

